            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> `vi{` along with `vi[` (and possibly other commands) move the cursor ahead and select a block that the cursor is not inside, rather than highlighting the block that the cursor is within. This is more easily explained with a screen recording:\r\n> \r\n> Is this a bug?\r\n\r\nNo, it is not. It is a [documented behavior](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/32b2db5ef9066f3d7ca375ea1ccc0fc44090727d/doc/mini-ai.txt#L424-L427) of first searching inside current line and only then inside neighborhood. As default search method is `'cover_or_next'`, it first searches for covering `{}` in current line. As there is none, 'mini.ai' tries to search for \"next\" `{}` in current line and finds it.\r\n\r\nIf you don't want for `a` / `i` textobjects from 'mini.ai' to be searched in the direction \"next\" and selected only if inside of them, use `search_method = 'cover'`. With a setup like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nrequire('mini.ai').setup({ search_method = 'cover' })\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith this setup, `vi{` will select the block you expect, while `vin{` can be used to select a \"next\" region (the `{ a: 2 }` in this example).\r\n\r\nClosing as duplicate of #392, #123, and #116.",
